7] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1045 "Finding a Basis of a Span" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120049 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "01aaa671-aae7-587d-86f6-117d15d3fbee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120524 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120524 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75588" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120524 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120524 #edb/ref 1071 "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ ${a}_{3},$ and ${a}_{4}$ are given below. Determine whether $B=\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}\\}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3},$ where ${a}_{4}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{4}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120524 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c72b3a4-9e18-57da-90d5-8d38d6a9dad9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120524 #edb/ref 1074 "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}\\}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}\\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=-8,$ ${x}_{2}=0,$ and ${x}_{3}=7,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n(-8)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+0\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+7\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-8+0+7=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120688 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120688 #edb/ref 1061 17504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120688 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "be88e95e-9faa-5bfe-b721-4eda45231dc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120688 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120689 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120689 #edb/ref 1061 17505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120689 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1169ef7f-a019-5cfa-8ef6-1d2ff514f2f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120689 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120752 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120752 #edb/ref 1061 23378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120752 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dee9e822-2a94-59f6-9320-08d94fcf8912" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120752 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120753 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120753 #edb/ref 1061 23379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120753 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "19b9d2e4-cc12-5198-97ee-5424e005fc32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120753 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120754 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120754 #edb/ref 1061 23546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120754 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3fd42240-5623-532c-921e-a37910ca748c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120754 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120755 #edb/ref 1061 23547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120755 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e1cd2039-adf1-5e16-9c01-414506e00c8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120755 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121040 #edb/ref 1064 "A **basis** of a subspace $U$ (in $\\mathbb{R}^n$) is a set of vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that is linearly independent and spans $U.$\n\nFor example, consider the following subspace consisting of the span of three vectors:\n![](images/aa/aaa76e82d8d54b305cfcfb0414f1fe7dd4d1c9d3ff932b4206a7b864922b394e.svg)\n\nTo find a basis of $U,$ we need to find a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $U.$\n\n**Watch out!** The set\n![](images/23/237305d1b82f44e0c365f721a33c35d8d983bd5facd4471d1885e8180ec7267e.svg)\nis *not* a basis of $U.$ It's true that this set *spans* $U,$ but the vectors in this set are *not* linearly independent. In particular, the vector $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2$ as follows:\n\n![](images/1d/1d0ff277adbace09315a1c0cd7c964eac41f5e2f080d0e32f888f379be435da0.svg)\n\nTo find a basis of $U = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\},$ we can start with $\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\},$ but we need to remove any \"redundant\" vectors that can be written as a linear combination of other vectors in the set. In this case, we remove ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3}$ because it can be written as a linear combination of $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}.$\n\nWe are left with the set\n\n![](images/79/79c529aa2fb5373ff9f8798300b1423ea63f90778bd4832ba1276f6af9c21f04.svg)\n\nThese two vectors are linearly independent (since they are not parallel), and removing a redundant vector does not change the span, so these vectors still span $U.$ That is to say,\n\n$$\nU = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\} = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\mathcal{B} = \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}$ is a basis of $U.$\n\n**Note:** To see that the vectors $\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}$ still span $U,$ first note that any vector $\\mathbf{b} \\in U$ can be written as a linear combination of the three original vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1 {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1} + x_2 {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} + x_3 {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3}\n$$\n\nSince ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} = -{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}+3{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2},$ then we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}(-{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2}) \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{3}){a}_{1}+({x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}){a}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\mathbf{b}$ can actually be written as a linear combination of only two vectors, ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that the set $\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}$ spans the subspace $U = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121040 #edb/ref 1065 3206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121040 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4ff21f5d-5007-5b74-b135-bdb080fd5541" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121040 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121064 #edb/ref 1064 "In the previous example, it was easy to see that one vector was a linear combination of the others. But what can we do when it's not so easy to tell? For example, consider the subspace\n\n![](images/4c/4c12a6e56a6cc7cd72371c59f41b33d8876cf4b98aa40766f0e67ad1bc3a32f7.svg)\n\nTo find a subset of linearly independent vectors, we can put the vectors in a matrix and compute the reduced row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent (or redundant) vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\n**But watch out!** We need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So, a basis of $U$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n![](images/1d/1dd0ca9bae9a29dea8fc34ed3d9fc453a1d52c48f1f19a95c2fb16c65cd9c7d8.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121064 #edb/ref 1065 3945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121064 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2c0f5ce4-8098-51b1-a25e-8cb4d84505c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121064 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding a Basis by Identifying Pivot Columns" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121065 #edb/ref 1064 "Earlier, we computed a basis of \n\n![](images/94/944c607bf892f0d346df79dfa08e6bfec699e64feac018d40a733be0a5c11041.svg)\n\nThe basis that we found was\n\n![](images/70/70a26164bd64f6e4483b38a08187cac2e1498df7f9066e2be9a295e7b04459da.svg)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}_3$ was not included in the basis. This means that $\\mathbf{a}_3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\nNow, what if we want to find the exact linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ that yields $\\mathbf{a}_3?$ That is to say, we want to find $x_1, x_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n![](images/2a/2a92f6d5b67012d342685436d6ccc0ae1670791a060129b10c7d858cafda7b26.svg)\n\nThis is equivalent to a system with the augmented matrix\n![](images/4d/4defcc004e360ec2e87798b158616575f284ae36369c766ee950c20ae06eff0a.svg)\n\nIf we reduced this matrix to the *reduced* row echelon form, we get\n\n![](images/1b/1be8cce3195aa633e76e855eba391dd8d1342cf0f3a19e0302eaf67e2828b909.svg)\n\nFinally, the third column of the *reduced* matrix tells us that $x_1=4$ and $x_2=5,$ meaning that\n![](images/cf/cf5185386d46084579b56fc6ad00769c7c5e7a0d2f320be7ccb28aa4961519ae.svg)\n\n**Watch out!** The matrix must be written in *reduced* row echelon form (with $1$'s for pivots and $0$'s above and below the pivots), not just row echelon form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121065 #edb/ref 1065 3968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121065 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "14b13853-b5c6-5bc9-825c-a11cd19dff93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121065 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding a Particular Linear Combination of Vectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246854 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246854 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246854 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246854 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186429766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246854 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "aa023706-5551-57d6-a234-fed6508928d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247170 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247170 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110850" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247170 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247170 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$  by inspection." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247170 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a861accd-0de4-505d-8847-8b30f8b4e546" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247170 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{3}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=0\\cdot {a}_{1}+(-1)\\cdot {a}_{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{3}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316712 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316712 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316712 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316712 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316712 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186429767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316712 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316712 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316712 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186429752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316712 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "484b2020-65a7-5604-96a1-0c5659cbed3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317510 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317510 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34456" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317510 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317510 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317510 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "585db78b-56e0-58b0-a767-e41e96247676" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317510 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{3}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342717 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342717 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342717 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3a085452-508a-5b7b-9027-923aa9e0f3a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342805 #edb/ref 1080 "12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342805 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342805 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "44c231fb-66cf-5e07-b090-8170edcc6e4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342943 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342943 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342943 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "549f247b-4a7b-5159-af5c-a77231e6302a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342981 #edb/ref 1080 "-5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342981 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342981 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "58