{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ by inspection." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429769 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "293eb96a-a168-5847-97d0-8739c8726172" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429769 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{1}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=1\\cdot {a}_{2}+1\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{1}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}},{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
